Reolink Integration for Hubitat

An open-source Hubitat integration for Reolink cameras, doorbells, NVRs, and Home Hubs.

Talks to Reolink devices over their local HTTP/JSON API. No cloud dependency, no Reolink account login required — just the device's IP (or the NVR/Home Hub's IP) and local credentials.

Why this exists

Reolink doesn't have an official Hubitat integration. This project fills that gap.

It's built and tested against real hardware rather than assumptions from the API docs alone. Several of the notes in this README exist specifically because Reolink's own documentation turned out to be incomplete, self-contradictory, or in one case outright buggy once checked against actual devices.

Features
Motion & AI detection — motion, person, vehicle, pet, package as native Hubitat attributes
Capability detection — a read-only supportedFeatures attribute per device, built from Reolink's own GetAbility API, tells you at a glance whether a specific device actually has PTZ, PTZ calibration, a spotlight, a siren, etc. Informational only — Hubitat has no way to hide commands on an individual device instance, so every command still shows on every device regardless; this just tells you ahead of time what's actually worth trying. Refreshable anytime via the Check Abilities command.
Doorbell button press — exposed as a standard PushableButton event, triggers Rule Machine like any other doorbell
Snapshot capture — pulls a still image via the camera's local API, cached on the hub so dashboard tiles don't hit the camera on every refresh
PTZ control — pan/tilt/zoom, plus preset recall (ptzGoToPreset), save (savePresetHere), and calibration
Spotlight, night vision, and siren control — on models that support them
Battery % — via the standard Hubitat Battery capability (see Known limitations — field names still unconfirmed against real battery hardware)
Sleep Status — tells "no response because asleep" apart from "no response because something's wrong"
Multi-source architecture — any mix of standalone cameras, NVRs, and Home Hubs at once, with per-device poll intervals
Adjustable logging — Errors Only (default), Normal, or Full, so you can dial up detail only when actively troubleshooting

Reolink's API has no dedicated "Home" command for PTZ cameras — ptzGoToPreset / savePresetHere are the practical equivalent.

Architecture

App (ParentApp.groovy) Owns all connection, auth, discovery, and polling logic. Maintains a list of sources — anything with its own IP and login: a standalone camera, an NVR, or a Home Hub.

Component drivers (CameraDriver.groovy, DoorbellDriver.groovy) Thin — no HTTP logic of their own. Every command delegates back to the parent app via parent.componentX(...).

Sources and channels A source with N paired channels (NVR/Home Hub) exposes channels 0..N-1. A standalone camera is a source with exactly one channel: 0. Each child device is tagged internally with (sourceId, channel).

Why it's built this way Moving a camera from standalone to behind an NVR/Home Hub later is a re-discovery and channel-remap — not a driver rewrite.

Polling A single central scheduler drives every device's poll/snapshot timing independently, self-heals after a hub reboot, and isolates one device's failure from affecting any other.

Installation
Via Hubitat Package Manager (HPM)
In HPM, choose Install → From a URL.
Point it at this repo's packageManifest.json.
HPM installs the app and both drivers together.
Manual install
Drivers Code → New Driver → paste CameraDriver.groovy → Save.
Repeat for DoorbellDriver.groovy.
Apps Code → New App → paste ParentApp.groovy → Save.
Apps → Add User App → search "Reolink Integration" (grouped under Integrations).
Setup
On the camera itself first — check its Network > Advanced (or Server) settings and make sure HTTP, HTTPS, and ONVIF are enabled.

These are often off by default on every model tested so far — not just Reolink's E1 line. This is the single most common reason a source fails to connect.

Open the app → Add a source... → enter the IP, port (default 443), username, and password.
Check "This is an NVR or Home Hub" if it has multiple channels.
Click into the new source — discovery runs automatically, no separate button click needed. Use "Re-run discovery" later if you pair a new camera to an NVR/Home Hub and need to refresh the channel list.
Create the device(s):
Single-channel (standalone) source — toggling the channel on creates the device immediately.
Multi-channel source (NVR/Home Hub) — select the channels you want, then toggle Create selected devices.
Check each device's new supportedFeatures attribute to see what that specific unit actually supports before trying PTZ/spotlight/siren commands.

See the in-app Tips, limitations & what works so far page (linked from the Sources list) for troubleshooting and device-specific quirks. It's kept current as things are discovered on real hardware, rather than duplicated here and left to drift out of sync.

Device compatibility
Model	Status
E1 Pro	✅ Working standalone — HTTP had to be manually enabled under Network > Advanced (off by default on this unit)
Trackmix	✅ Working standalone — full PTZ confirmed
RLC-1240A	✅ Working standalone
RLC-410W	✅ Working standalone — older/basic model, predates AI detection entirely (motion only, no person/vehicle/pet)
Duo 3V PoE / Duo 3 PoE	✅ Working standalone — has a built-in spotlight
E1 Outdoor	✅ Working standalone, full PTZ confirmed — ⚠️ some ~2021-era firmware units have a known bug causing intermittent false "asleep" reports; confirmed absent on newer (2024-era) firmware. See Known limitations
Reolink Video Doorbell WiFi-W (wired, not battery)	✅ Working standalone — package detection confirmed
Doorbell 2K Gen 2	❌ No local HTTP/ONVIF server standalone, confirmed — battery-class device, requires a Home Hub/NVR
Battery-class (Argus line, Doorbell Battery, Gen 2 doorbells)	❌ No local HTTP/ONVIF server, confirmed — see Known limitations
Base E1, Lumus	❓ Untested — Reolink's docs claim no local HTTP support, unconfirmed
Home Hub Pro	❓ Untested — needed to confirm NVR-style channel importing/selection and per-channel capability behavior
Argus Pro (Black)	❓ Untested — needed to confirm WiFi battery relay behavior through a Hub at a distance, and battery-status detection

PoE cameras and WiFi cameras outside the E1/battery lines are expected to work standalone without issue.

Known limitations

Battery-class devices Argus line, Doorbell Battery, Gen 2 doorbells. This is a deliberate, permanent firmware exclusion across Reolink's entire battery-powered product line — confirmed via Reolink's own community forum, not a timing/sleep issue and not something that varies by power source. Even a battery-class device running continuously on a DC adapter (not just trickle-charging) still has no local API, because the firmware itself never includes that server stack on battery models. Only reachable once paired to a Home Hub or NVR.

E1 / E1 Pro / Lumus Reolink's own documentation is inconsistent about which of these support local HTTP/HTTPS. Don't trust the model name — check the camera's own Network > Advanced (or Server) settings directly.

Known older-firmware bug: false "asleep" on some E1 Outdoor units Some E1-series cameras on ~2021-era firmware have a documented bug (independently confirmed via community reports and Reolink's own support docs) where the camera's web server intermittently returns corrupted/garbled data instead of a real response — not encryption, not a real connectivity problem, just bad data from the camera itself. Confirmed via testing: several garbled responses captured were byte-for-byte identical across completely different login sessions hours apart, which real per-session encryption could never produce, ruling that out in favor of a firmware bug. The integration automatically retries once when this specific failure is detected, which helps but doesn't fully eliminate it since the underlying bug is intermittent. See the in-app Tips page for recognition and remediation steps (toggle HTTP/HTTPS + reboot first, firmware update or a Reolink support request if that doesn't help).

Capability detection (supportedFeatures) is informational only Built from real GetAbility data gathered across 8+ devices spanning multiple models and firmware years. It does not hide or disable any command — Hubitat can't do that for an individual device instance. A few fields required correction after real-world testing contradicted the initial mapping (notably PTZ Calibration and Spotlight detection both needed a different underlying field than first assumed) — this is expected to keep refining as more hardware gets tested. Package detection is only confirmed on doorbells (cameras don't have it); battery-status detection via GetAbility is not yet wired in (the existing GetBatteryInfo-based detection remains the source of truth, unconfirmed against real battery hardware); NVR/Home Hub per-channel ability behavior is untested — everything confirmed so far is a directly-connected standalone camera.

Motion/AI detection field names GetAiState/GetMdState parsing (motion, person, vehicle, pet, package, doorbell button press) is built from a consistent API pattern but hasn't yet been confirmed against a real triggered event on hardware — GetAbility testing has confirmed which AI categories a given device supports, but not that the live-trigger field parsing itself fires correctly during an actual motion/person/vehicle event. If motion/AI attributes aren't updating as expected, this is the first place to check — see parseReolinkState in the driver files.

Unverified commands SetPtzPreset, SetWhiteLed, SetIrLights, AudioAlarmPlay, GetBatteryInfo are built from consistent patterns across independent Reolink API references, but actual command execution isn't yet confirmed against every model (capability presence is, via supportedFeatures — that's a different thing from confirming the command works when called). See TODO comments in the app source for specifics.

Security note

Camera/NVR/Hub credentials (username and password) are stored in the app's state, unencrypted — this is standard practice for Hubitat community apps, since the platform has no built-in secrets vault, but it's worth knowing before entering credentials for anything you wouldn't want readable from the hub's own backup/state data.

Contributing / testing

Testing against additional hardware is genuinely useful, particularly:

A Home Hub/NVR — multi-channel discovery, channel importing, per-channel capability behavior
Argus Pro or any true battery-class device paired to a Hub/NVR — confirming relay-through-sleep behavior and real battery-status detection
Any device with a true external/accessory floodlight (not the built-in spotlight most models have) — the top-level floodLight ability field has shown unreliable/absent on every device tested so far, including confirmed spotlight-equipped ones, and hasn't been tested against genuine floodlight hardware

A standalone test tool for dumping a device's raw GetAbility data (no connection to the production app, safe to run against any camera) is available in this repo's Tests - Groovy RAW folder if you want to contribute capability data for a model not yet covered above.

